Array [
  Object {
    "description": "Create new fiori elements page like ListReport, ObjectPage, CustomPage",
    "functionalityId": "add-page",
  },
  Object {
    "description": "Remove existing fiori elements page from the application",
    "functionalityId": "delete-page",
  },
  Object {
    "description": "Add new controller extension by creating javascript or typescript file and updates manifest.json with entry. Controller extensions allow users to extensiate default behaviour with custom controllers code.",
    "functionalityId": "create-controller-extension",
  },
  Object {
    "description": "Inserts a SAP Fiori elements building block (FilterBar, Table, Chart, Form, Field, RichTextEditor, Page, or an aggregation child: CustomColumn, Action, CustomFilterField, CustomFormField, RichTextEditorButtonGroups) into an existing view or fragment XML file. For FilterBar, Table, Form: the required annotation shell (com.sap.vocabularies.UI.v1.SelectionFields, com.sap.vocabularies.UI.v1.LineItem, com.sap.vocabularies.UI.v1.FieldGroup) is written automatically to the app annotation file — you do NOT need to check or read the annotation file first. If the annotation already exists it will not be duplicated. Call execute_functionality exactly once per building block — do NOT retry or call it again after a successful result. If the user request is ambiguous (e.g. multiple elements of the same type exist at the same level — such as two macros:Table or two macros:FilterBar in the same parent — and the target is unclear), ask the user to clarify by id (e.g. \\"which table — Table or Table1?\\") before calling execute_functionality. Do NOT read any files to resolve ambiguity. Use search_docs with a building block type name (e.g. \\"FilterBar building block\\", \\"Table building block\\") to retrieve full property documentation from bb_macro_api.md before populating buildingBlockData. Types requiring UI5 >= 1.117.0: RichTextEditor, RichTextEditorButtonGroups. Types requiring UI5 >= 1.136.0: Page (basic). Types requiring UI5 >= 1.145.0: Page (full layout). RichTextEditor and RichTextEditorButtonGroups must be placed in a fragment file (custom section), NOT in a view file. If the app has no fragment files, tell the user: \\"RichTextEditor can only be added to a custom section fragment. Please create a custom section fragment first, then ask again.\\" When adding RichTextEditorButtonGroups to a RichTextEditor that already has button groups, always call get_functionality_details again first to get the current aggregationPath — do not reuse a path from a previous call. Page building block must be placed in a custom page view file (.view.xml), NOT in a fragment file. If the app has no custom page view files, tell the user: \\"Page building block can only be added to a custom page view. Please create a custom page view first, then ask again.\\" Only one macros:Page element is allowed per view. If a macros:Page already exists in the view, do NOT add another — instead add building blocks (e.g. Table, FilterBar) inside it using the XPath that points to the macros:Page items aggregation. When adding a Page building block, always use the XPath that is a direct child of /mvc:View as aggregationPath (e.g. /mvc:View/Page) — never use a deeper path like /mvc:View/Page/content. Aggregation child types (CustomColumn, Action, CustomFilterField, CustomFormField, RichTextEditorButtonGroups) insert into a parent macro aggregation — their aggregationPath must point to the parent macro element (CustomColumn → Table, Action → Table, CustomFilterField → FilterBar, CustomFormField → Form, RichTextEditorButtonGroups → RichTextEditorWithMetadata). For aggregation children: if the user specifies a position (before/after a specific existing column/field), pass position as { anchor: \\"<existingElementKey>\\", placement: \\"Before\\"|\\"After\\" } — the anchor value is the key attribute of the existing element in the XML. Keys are always kebab-case derived from the label (e.g. label \\"Test Header\\" → key=\\"test-header\\", label \\"Product Name\\" → key=\\"product-name\\"), so you can derive the anchor from the label without reading the file. Otherwise omit position and the column/field is appended at the end. Fragment and event handler options for aggregation children (CustomColumn, Action, CustomFilterField, CustomFormField, RichTextEditorButtonGroups): (1) New fragment, no handler — omit embededFragment entirely; a new fragment file is created automatically. (2) New fragment, with handler — set embededFragment.eventHandler to true (auto-generates a standalone .js handler named after the fragment with a default onPress function) or { fileName: \\"MyHandler\\", fnName: \\"myFn\\" } (auto-generates a standalone .js handler with a specific name and function). Do NOT create handler files manually. (3) Existing fragment, no handler — set embededFragment.name to the existing fragment file name; no handler is generated. (4) Existing fragment, with handler — set embededFragment.name to the existing fragment file name and embededFragment.eventHandler to { fileName: \\"ExistingHandler\\", fnName: \\"myFn\\" }. Ask the user which case applies before calling execute_functionality if it is unclear. Action handler options: (1) New handler file, new function — set embeddedAction.eventHandler to { fileName: \\"MyHandler\\", fnName: \\"myFn\\" }. The tool creates the file automatically. (2) Existing handler file, new function — set embeddedAction.name to the existing file and embeddedAction.eventHandler to { fileName: \\"ExistingFile\\", fnName: \\"newFn\\" }. The tool wires the XML but does NOT append the function to the existing file. After execute_functionality, append the new function to the handler .js file. (3) Existing handler file, existing function — set embeddedAction.name to the existing file and embeddedAction.eventHandler to { fileName: \\"ExistingFile\\", fnName: \\"existingFn\\" }. The function already exists — no file editing needed. FilterBar wiring: add the FilterBar first (note its id), then when adding the Table pass buildingBlockData.filterBar set to that id. If the Table already exists and needs linking, edit the view XML directly to add filterBar=\\"<filterBarId>\\" to the macros:Table element. metaPath format for Table and FilterBar — never set contextPath, always leave it unset: (1) Relative binding (navigation property as value source, e.g. \\"to_Booking\\"): metaPath=\\"<navProp>/@<annotation>#<qualifier>\\" — no leading slash. Example: metaPath=\\"to_Booking/@com.sap.vocabularies.UI.v1.LineItem#tableMacro\\". (2) Absolute binding (entity set as value source, e.g. \\"Booking\\"): metaPath=\\"/<entitySet>/@<annotation>#<qualifier>\\" — leading slash. Example: metaPath=\\"/Booking/@com.sap.vocabularies.UI.v1.SelectionFields#filterBarMacro1\\".",
    "functionalityId": "add-building-block",
  },
